<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ location.primary_place_name }} Weather — Glossary</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ nav.css }}">
</head>
<body>
<header class="site-header">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a class="brand" href="{{ nav.home }}">{{ location.primary_place_name }} Weather</a>
    <nav>
      <a href="{{ nav.home }}">Today</a>
      <a href="{{ nav.archive }}">Archive</a>
      <a href="{{ nav.accuracy }}">Accuracy</a>
      <a href="{{ nav.glossary }}">Glossary</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
<main class="wrap">
  <p class="disclaimer-banner">Experimental, AI-assisted forecast &mdash; not an official government product. Do not rely on this for life-safety decisions.{% if location.local_bulletin_source_name %} For official warnings and advisories, consult {% if location.local_bulletin_url %}<a href="{{ location.local_bulletin_url }}">{{ location.local_bulletin_source_name }}</a>{% else %}{{ location.local_bulletin_source_name }}{% endif %}.{% endif %}</p>

  <h1>Glossary</h1>
  <p class="meta">The forecast uses technical terms where a plainer word would lose something. These are what they mean. Where a definition carries numbers set by a standards body, that body is named &mdash; the rest are this project's own plain-English wording.</p>

  <dl class="glossary">
  {% for entry in glossary %}
    <dt id="{{ entry.slug }}">{{ entry.term }}</dt>
    <dd>
      {{ entry.definition }}
      {% if entry.source %}<span class="source">Source: {{ entry.source }}</span>{% endif %}
    </dd>
  {% endfor %}
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">If the forecast uses a word that is not here, that is a defect worth reporting &mdash; see <a href="{{ nav.github }}">the repository</a>.</p>
</main>
</body>
</html>
